  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L09_002_Build A Dictionary Step By Step",
   "display_name": "Build A Dictionary Step By Step",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Like a list, a dictionary can be built step by step. Start with the <b>empty dictionary</b> <code>{}</code>, then add pairs by assigning to a new key:</p>\n<pre>d[key] = value</pre>\n<pre>cubes = {}\nfor k in range(1, 4):\n    cubes[k] = k * k * k\nprint(cubes)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>{1: 1, 2: 8, 3: 27}</pre>\n<p>Keys do not have to be strings: here they are integers.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>squares_dict(n)</code>, which returns a dictionary with the keys <code>1, 2, ..., n</code>, where each key <code>k</code> has the value <code>k * k</code>. For <code>n = 0</code>, return the empty dictionary.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>squares_dict(3)</code> returns <code>{1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def squares_dict(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(squares_dict(3))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(squares_dict(3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(squares_dict(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(squares_dict(1))",
