  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L09_003_Dictionary From Pairs",
   "display_name": "Dictionary From Pairs",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>dict(...)</code> converts a list of <code>(key, value)</code> tuples into a dictionary. Going back, <code>list(d.items())</code> gives the pairs as a list of tuples:</p>\n<pre>pairs = [(&quot;Goa&quot;, &quot;Panaji&quot;), (&quot;Kerala&quot;, &quot;Thiruvananthapuram&quot;)]\ncapitals = dict(pairs)\nprint(capitals[&quot;Goa&quot;])\nprint(list(capitals.items()))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Panaji\n[(&#x27;Goa&#x27;, &#x27;Panaji&#x27;), (&#x27;Kerala&#x27;, &#x27;Thiruvananthapuram&#x27;)]</pre>\n<p>A key can appear only <b>once</b> in a dictionary. If a key is repeated, the <b>last</b> value wins:</p>\n<pre>print(dict([(&quot;a&quot;, 1), (&quot;b&quot;, 2), (&quot;a&quot;, 3)]))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>{&#x27;a&#x27;: 3, &#x27;b&#x27;: 2}</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>from_pairs(pairs)</code>, which returns a dictionary made from the list of <code>(key, value)</code> tuples <code>pairs</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>from_pairs([(\"x\", 1), (\"y\", 2)])</code> returns <code>{'x': 1, 'y': 2}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def from_pairs(pairs):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(from_pairs([(\"x\", 1), (\"y\", 2)]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(from_pairs([(\"x\", 1), (\"y\", 2)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{'x': 1, 'y': 2}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(from_pairs([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{}",
