   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L09_004_Keys Must Be Immutable",
   "display_name": "Keys Must Be Immutable",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A dictionary key must be <b>immutable</b>: numbers, strings and <b>tuples</b> are fine, but a <b>list cannot be a key</b> (it could change later, and then Python could not find it):</p>\n<pre>d = {[1, 2]: &quot;no&quot;}</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">TypeError: unhashable type: &#x27;list&#x27;</pre>\n<p>(Values can be anything, including lists.) Tuples make good keys for positions on a board:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 1px;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">board =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">(0, 0)</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">(0, 1)</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">(1, 1)</td><td style=\"padding-left:8px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">key<br><br>value</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">&#x27;X&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">&#x27;O&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">&#x27;X&#x27;</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">only the filled cells are stored; the key is the (row, column) tuple</div>\n<pre>board = {(0, 0): &quot;X&quot;, (0, 1): &quot;O&quot;, (1, 1): &quot;X&quot;}\nprint(board[(0, 1)])\nprint((1, 0) in board)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>O\nFalse</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>cell(board, r, c)</code>, where <code>board</code> is a dictionary like the one above. Return the value stored for the position <code>(r, c)</code>, or <code>\".\"</code> if that position is not in the dictionary.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>cell({(0, 0): \"X\", (0, 1): \"O\"}, 0, 1)</code> returns <code>\"O\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def cell(board, r, c):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(cell({(0, 0): \"X\", (0, 1): \"O\"}, 0, 1))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
