   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L09_005_Access And KeyError",
   "display_name": "Access And KeyError",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>d[key]</code> gives the value for <code>key</code>. If the key is not in the dictionary, you get a <code>KeyError</code> (the dictionary version of <code>IndexError</code>):</p>\n<pre>menu = {&quot;tea&quot;: 10, &quot;coffee&quot;: 25}\nprint(menu[&quot;tea&quot;])\nprint(&quot;juice&quot; in menu)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>10\nFalse</pre>\n<pre>print(menu[&quot;juice&quot;])</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">KeyError: &#x27;juice&#x27;</pre>\n<p><code>key in d</code> checks whether a key is present (it checks <b>keys</b>, not values).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>price_of(menu, item)</code>, which returns the price of <code>item</code> from the dictionary <code>menu</code>, or <code>-1</code> if the item is not on the menu.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>price_of({\"tea\": 10, \"coffee\": 25}, \"coffee\")</code> returns <code>25</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def price_of(menu, item):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(price_of({\"tea\": 10, \"coffee\": 25}, \"coffee\"))\n",
