  {
   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "L09_008_Finding A Key By Value",
   "display_name": "Finding A Key By Value",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Looking up a value by its key is fast and easy. The other way round (which key has this value?) needs a loop over the keys:</p>\n<pre>ages = {&quot;Asha&quot;: 19, &quot;Ravi&quot;: 20, &quot;Meera&quot;: 19}\nfor name in ages:            # a for loop over a dictionary gives its keys\n    print(name, ages[name])</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Asha 19\nRavi 20\nMeera 19</pre>\n<p>Dictionaries keep their pairs in the order they were added.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>find_key(d, v)</code>, which returns the <b>first</b> key (in the dictionary's order) whose value equals <code>v</code>, or <code>None</code> if no key has that value.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>find_key({\"Asha\": 19, \"Ravi\": 20, \"Meera\": 19}, 19)</code> returns <code>\"Asha\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def find_key(d, v):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(find_key({\"Asha\": 19, \"Ravi\": 20, \"Meera\": 19}, 19))\n",
