  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L09_010_Removing With pop And del",
   "display_name": "Removing With pop And del",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>There are several ways to remove pairs:</p>\n<ul><li><code>d.pop(key)</code> removes the pair and <b>returns</b> its value (a <code>KeyError</code> if the key is missing);</li><li><code>d.pop(key, default)</code> returns <code>default</code> instead of failing when the key is missing;</li><li><code>del d[key]</code> removes the pair without returning anything;</li><li><code>d.clear()</code> removes everything.</li></ul>\n<pre>d = {&quot;a&quot;: 1, &quot;b&quot;: 2, &quot;c&quot;: 3}\nx = d.pop(&quot;b&quot;)\nprint(x, d)\nprint(d.pop(&quot;zzz&quot;, None))\ndel d[&quot;a&quot;]\nprint(d)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>2 {&#x27;a&#x27;: 1, &#x27;c&#x27;: 3}\nNone\n{&#x27;c&#x27;: 3}</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>take(d, key)</code>, which removes <code>key</code> from the dictionary <code>d</code> and returns its value; if the key is not there, it returns <code>None</code> and changes nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}</code>, <code>take(d, \"a\")</code> returns <code>1</code> and <code>d</code> becomes <code>{'b': 2}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define take(d, key).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\n# print(take(d, \"a\"))\n# print(d)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nprint(take(d, \"a\"))\nprint(d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1\n{'b': 2}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {\"a\": 1}\nprint(take(d, \"z\"), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "None {'a': 1}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {1: \"x\", 2: \"y\"}\nprint(take(d, 2), take(d, 2), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "y None {1: 'x'}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {}\nprint(take(d, 0), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "None {}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L09_011_The Last Pair With popitem",
   "display_name": "The Last Pair With popitem",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>d.popitem()</code> removes the <b>most recently added</b> pair and returns it as a tuple <code>(key, value)</code>. It is handy when you want to take pairs out one at a time, like a stack.</p>\n<pre>d = {&quot;a&quot;: 1, &quot;b&quot;: 2, &quot;c&quot;: 3}\nprint(d.popitem())\nprint(d)\nd[&quot;z&quot;] = 26\nprint(d.popitem())</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(&#x27;c&#x27;, 3)\n{&#x27;a&#x27;: 1, &#x27;b&#x27;: 2}\n(&#x27;z&#x27;, 26)</pre>\n<p>(<code>popitem()</code> on an empty dictionary is a <code>KeyError</code>.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>undo(d, n)</code>, which removes the last <code>n</code> added pairs from the dictionary <code>d</code> and returns a list of the removed <code>(key, value)</code> tuples, in the order they were removed. If <code>d</code> has fewer than <code>n</code> pairs, remove them all.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}</code>, <code>undo(d, 2)</code> returns <code>[('c', 3), ('b', 2)]</code> and <code>d</code> becomes <code>{'a': 1}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define undo(d, n): use popitem() in a loop.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\n# print(undo(d, 2))\n# print(d)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\nprint(undo(d, 2))\nprint(d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('c', 3), ('b', 2)]\n{'a': 1}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {\"a\": 1}\nprint(undo(d, 5), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('a', 1)] {}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}\nprint(undo(d, 0), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[] {1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = {\"x\": 0, \"y\": 1}\nprint(undo(d, 2), d)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('y', 1), ('x', 0)] {}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L09_012_Merging Dictionaries",
   "display_name": "Merging Dictionaries",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>d.update(other)</code> copies all the pairs of the dictionary <code>other</code> into <code>d</code>. Keys that are in both get the value from <code>other</code>. Like list methods, <code>update</code> changes <code>d</code> itself.</p>\n<p>To get a <b>new</b> dictionary and keep the original, first make a copy with <code>d.copy()</code> (just as with lists, <code>e = d</code> would only give a second name for the same dictionary).</p>\n<pre>prices = {&quot;tea&quot;: 10, &quot;coffee&quot;: 25}\nnew_prices = prices.copy()\nnew_prices.update({&quot;tea&quot;: 12, &quot;juice&quot;: 30})\nprint(new_prices)\nprint(prices)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>{&#x27;tea&#x27;: 12, &#x27;coffee&#x27;: 25, &#x27;juice&#x27;: 30}\n{&#x27;tea&#x27;: 10, &#x27;coffee&#x27;: 25}</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>merged(d1, d2)</code>, which returns a new dictionary with all pairs of <code>d1</code> and <code>d2</code>; for keys in both, the value from <code>d2</code> wins. Neither <code>d1</code> nor <code>d2</code> may change.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>merged({\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}, {\"b\": 20, \"c\": 3})</code> returns <code>{'a': 1, 'b': 20, 'c': 3}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define merged(d1, d2): copy, then update.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(merged({\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}, {\"b\": 20, \"c\": 3}))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(merged({\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}, {\"b\": 20, \"c\": 3}))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{'a': 1, 'b': 20, 'c': 3}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "a = {\"x\": 1}\nb = {}\nprint(merged(a, b), a, b)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{'x': 1} {'x': 1} {}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "a = {\"k\": 1}\nb = {\"k\": 2}\nm = merged(a, b)\nprint(m, a, b)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{'k': 2} {'k': 1} {'k': 2}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(merged({}, {\"z\": 0}))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{'z': 0}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 13,
   "xml_name": "L09_013_A Changed Copy",
   "display_name": "A Changed Copy",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To make a changed version of a dictionary and keep the original, copy it first (or build a new dictionary in a loop), then change the copy.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>with_bonus(marks, bonus)</code>, where <code>marks</code> is a dictionary from names to integer marks. Return a new dictionary with the same names, where each mark is increased by <code>bonus</code>. <code>marks</code> must not change.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>m = {\"Asha\": 80, \"Ravi\": 65}</code>, <code>with_bonus(m, 5)</code> returns <code>{'Asha': 85, 'Ravi': 70}</code> and <code>m</code> is unchanged.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define with_bonus(marks, bonus): return a NEW dictionary.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# m = {\"Asha\": 80, \"Ravi\": 65}\n# print(with_bonus(m, 5))\n# print(m)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
