  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L09_015_Looping Over Items",
   "display_name": "Looping Over Items",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Three methods give views of a dictionary's contents:</p>\n<ul><li><code>d.keys()</code>: the keys;</li><li><code>d.values()</code>: the values;</li><li><code>d.items()</code>: the <code>(key, value)</code> pairs, which a <code>for</code> loop can <b>unpack</b>.</li></ul>\n<pre>marks = {&quot;Asha&quot;: 82, &quot;Ravi&quot;: 67, &quot;Meera&quot;: 91}\nprint(list(marks.keys()))\nprint(list(marks.values()))\nfor name, m in marks.items():\n    print(name, m)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;Asha&#x27;, &#x27;Ravi&#x27;, &#x27;Meera&#x27;]\n[82, 67, 91]\nAsha 82\nRavi 67\nMeera 91</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>above(marks, cutoff)</code>, which returns a list of the names in the dictionary <code>marks</code> whose marks are <b>more than</b> <code>cutoff</code>, in the dictionary's order. Use <code>items()</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>above({\"Asha\": 82, \"Ravi\": 67, \"Meera\": 91}, 80)</code> returns <code>['Asha', 'Meera']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define above(marks, cutoff): loop with items().\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(above({\"Asha\": 82, \"Ravi\": 67, \"Meera\": 91}, 80))\n",
