  {
   "xml_index": 16,
   "xml_name": "L09_016_Key With The Largest Value",
   "display_name": "Key With The Largest Value",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To find the key with the largest value, loop over the pairs and remember the best one so far (as you did with lists). Start with the first key, which you can get with <code>list(d.keys())[0]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>sorted(d)</code> gives a sorted <b>list of the keys</b>.</p>\n<pre>d = {&quot;b&quot;: 3, &quot;a&quot;: 7, &quot;c&quot;: 5}\nprint(sorted(d))\nprint(list(d.keys())[0])</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;]\nb</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>best(d)</code>, which returns the key with the largest value in the non-empty dictionary <code>d</code>. If several keys share the largest value, return the one that comes <b>first</b> in the dictionary's order.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>best({\"b\": 3, \"a\": 7, \"c\": 7})</code> returns <code>\"a\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define best(d).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(best({\"b\": 3, \"a\": 7, \"c\": 7}))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(best({\"b\": 3, \"a\": 7, \"c\": 7}))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "a",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(best({\"only\": -1}))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "only",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
