  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "L09_019_Grouping Into Lists",
   "display_name": "Grouping Into Lists",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A dictionary's values can be <b>lists</b>. That lets us <b>group</b> things: the key says which group, and the list holds its members.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 1px;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">groups =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:4px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;background:#cfdcec;color:#1f3a63;border-radius:2px 2px 0 0\">2</td><td style=\"padding-left:8px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">key<br><br>value</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">[&#x27;cat&#x27;, &#x27;dog&#x27;]</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">[&#x27;lion&#x27;]</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:0 0 2px 2px\">[&#x27;ox&#x27;]</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">words grouped by their length</div>\n<p>For each item: if its group is new, start it with an empty list; then append the item.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>group_by_length(words)</code>, which returns a dictionary from each word length to the list of words (from the list <code>words</code>) with that length, in their original order. The keys appear in the order their first word appears.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>group_by_length([\"cat\", \"lion\", \"dog\", \"ox\"])</code> returns <code>{3: ['cat', 'dog'], 4: ['lion'], 2: ['ox']}</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define group_by_length(words).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(group_by_length([\"cat\", \"lion\", \"dog\", \"ox\"]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(group_by_length([\"cat\", \"lion\", \"dog\", \"ox\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "{3: ['cat', 'dog'], 4: ['lion'], 2: ['ox']}",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
