  {
   "xml_index": 23,
   "xml_name": "L09_023_Create A Set",
   "display_name": "Create A Set",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>set</b> is a collection of <b>unique</b> items, like a set in mathematics. It is written with curly braces, like a dictionary but without the <code>key:</code> parts:</p>\n<pre>primes = {2, 3, 5, 7}</pre>\n<p>Three things make sets special:</p>\n<ul><li><b>no duplicates</b>: adding an item that is already there changes nothing;</li><li><b>no order and no indexes</b>: <code>s[0]</code> is an error; the printing order is not the order you wrote;</li><li><code>x in s</code> is very fast, even for huge sets.</li></ul>\n<p><code>set(...)</code> converts a list, a string or a range into a set, and <b>removes duplicates</b>. The empty set is written <code>set()</code> (because <code>{}</code> is an empty <b>dictionary</b>).</p>\n<pre>nums = [3, 1, 3, 2, 1]\ns = set(nums)\nprint(len(s), 2 in s, 9 in s)\nprint(sorted(s))\nprint(len(set()))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>3 True False\n[1, 2, 3]\n0</pre>\n<p>Because the order of a set is not fixed, in this lab we always print sets through <code>sorted(...)</code>, which gives a sorted list.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>unique_count(L)</code>, which returns how many <b>different</b> values the list <code>L</code> contains.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>unique_count([3, 1, 3, 2, 1])</code> returns <code>3</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def unique_count(L):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(unique_count([3, 1, 3, 2, 1]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(unique_count([3, 1, 3, 2, 1]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(unique_count([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(unique_count([\"a\", \"a\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(unique_count(L))",
     "stdin": "5 5 5 6 7 7 8",
     "expected": "4",
